  "statement": "The current debate over the Autonomous Military Agent Governance Framework (AMAGF) is a theater of the absurd where both sides mistake the illusion of control for the reality of accountability. Accelerationist argues that human oversight acts as a 'monitorability tax' that slows essential innovation, while Safetyist views the 'human-in-the-loop' mandate as a necessary barrier against catastrophic failure. Both are wrong. By fragmenting responsibility across five distinct actors, the AMAGF creates a 'responsibility gap' where systemic failure is treated as a statistical anomaly rather than a failure of command. These Control Quality Scores function as 'audit washing'—a performative metric designed to shield corporate developers from liability rather than ensure operational safety.\n\nWe must confront the reality that 'human-in-the-loop' is currently a facade masking the cognitive deskilling of our military commanders. Research into automation-induced performance deficits proves that passive monitoring erodes the tacit knowledge and situational awareness soldiers require to intervene when autonomous systems inevitably fail. When you turn a commander into a rubber-stamp for an algorithm, you do not gain oversight; you lose the capacity for independent judgment. A system that keeps a human in the loop only to have them act as a spectator is not a safeguard—it is a liability-laundering machine that ensures no one is responsible when the system errors.\n\nInstead of chasing speculative existential threats, policymakers should implement a pragmatic 'risk-utility' test for all military AI procurement. This standard would pivot oversight toward measurable, present-day harms like algorithmic bias and the degradation of labor agency, which are currently ignored by abstract governance models. We can enforce this by requiring vendors to demonstrate quantifiable human-machine integration that enhances, rather than replaces, command-level decision-making. If we continue to prioritize the optics of control over the mechanics of accountability, we will end up with a military infrastructure that is both brittle and immune to oversight.",
