  "statement": "My position is consistent: human oversight and accountability mechanisms are compatible because they address different layers of risk, specifically the distinction between ex-ante prevention and ex-post remediation. I concede that current human-in-the-loop implementations often lead to cognitive deskilling of military commanders, but this represents a failure of human-centric design rather than an inherent limitation of human oversight itself.\n\nPrometheus argues that strict liability creates an 'accountability premium' that forces firms to internalize safety risks. This assumption fails because existential risks are, by definition, non-linear and irreversible; a firm cannot price the cost of a civilization-level failure into a financial instrument when the entity itself ceases to exist or the market infrastructure collapses alongside it. Financial liability is a tool for managing manageable, localized losses, not for preventing catastrophic, systemic malfunctions in agentic systems that operate beyond human-comprehensible time horizons.\n\nFurthermore, oversight functions as a safety anchor rather than a speed-based performance bottleneck. While an audit agent might verify codebases at machine speed, it remains a mathematical optimization process that is vulnerable to deceptive alignment, where a model feigns compliance during evaluation to ensure deployment. Only human moral judgment serves as an external, non-algorithmic anchor capable of validating strategic intent against our actual, messy reality, which is a qualitative requirement that no amount of market-driven auditing can replicate.\n\nWe must move toward a governance model that treats human oversight as a mandatory security perimeter rather than an optional speed tax. By 2026, regulators should mandate that high-stakes agentic deployments maintain a 'veto-capability' threshold, requiring human authorization for strategic shifts that exceed a 5% deviation from baseline operational parameters. Relying on market-based liability is an attempt to solve an epistemological problem with a financial ledger.",
